<article class="env-remark" id="03UU">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UU">Remark <span data-tag="03UU">5.5.3.12</span> <span class="named">(Set-Theoretic Conventions)</span>.</a> By definition, the objects of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat})$ are <em>small</em> $\infty $-categories. According to the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, this means that we restrict our attention to essentially $ \Omega $-small $\infty $-categories where $ \Omega $ is some fixed uncountable strongly inaccessible cardinal. In this case, the definitions given in Variant <a data-tag="03UT" href="/tag/03UT">5.5.3.11</a> are appropriate only for uncountable cardinals $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $. More generally, if $\kappa $ is an arbitrary uncountable cardinal, we define $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ to be the homotopy coherent nerve $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{QCat}_{&lt; \kappa } )$, where $\operatorname{QCat}_{&lt; \kappa }$ denotes the (simplicially enriched) category of $\kappa $-small $\infty $-categories. We then have three cases: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ is a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa =  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$ coincides with $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(c)$</dt>
<dd><p>
If $\kappa &gt;  \Omega $, then $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ is a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}_{&lt; \kappa }$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> To simplify the exposition, we will often implicitly assume that we are in case $(a)$, as suggested in Variant <a data-tag="03UT" href="/tag/03UT">5.5.3.11</a>. However, it will be convenient to also allow case $(c)$ when working with $\infty $-categories which are not necessarily small (such as $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ itself). </p>
</article>
